  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nFog should quickly clear up by mid-morning with another hot and\nhumid day ahead. 850mb temperatures of +17C to +19C support \nhighs reaching the upper 80s to lower 90s south of the \nmountains, and combining these with dewpoints in the upper 60s \nto low 70s will push heat indices into the 90s for much of \nnorthern New England. The peak of the heat is expected to be \nacross southeastern NH extending into interior western Maine and\nthe western ME coast, where a Heat Advisory remains in effect \nfor heat indices expected to be in the 95F-100F range. We should\nsee some increase in diurnal cu in the afternoon, but the 500mb\npattern indicates weak shortwave ridging building in, which \nshould add some subsidence to keep any convection very isolated \nmuch of the day. This weak ridge does begin to shift east toward\nthis evening, so we'll see if any convection develops in NH or \nmoves in from the west, similar to last evening.\n\nFor tonight, temperatures remain mild ranging from the mid 60s \nto around 70. Latest guidance is in reasonably good agreement in\nshortwave energy approaching from the west overnight, which \nwill result in a chance of showers along with an increase in \nclouds. Valley fog is again possible, but the clouds may end up \nhindering this potential. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA warm and humid airmass remains in place for Saturday with\nhigher chances for showers and storms with better defined \nshortwaves crossing northern New England. 00Z guidance is \ncoming into better agreement with scattered showers possible in \nthe morning with the same wave that approaches the region Friday\nnight. A quicker exit to the east may bring a break and a \nbetter chance for some degrees of clearing and instability to \nbuild going into the afternoon hours for showers and \nthunderstorms to develop with the arrival of the next shortwave.\nThe latest NAMnest, RAP, and HRRR are generally supportive of \nthis thinking, although the amount of instability/clear is still\na bit uncertain (and to that extent high temperatures). If \nenough instability is gained, a few strong to severe storms will\nbe possible given 30-35 kt of deep layer shear. PWATs are still\nexpected to be around 1.75\" so rain rates could again be quite \nefficient, and localized flooding is possible in areas that \nreceive persistent or multiple rounds of convection. Back to \nhigh temperatures...model consensus is mostly in the 80s for \nmost areas, although parts of NH could still approach 90. Once \nshowers and storms some to an end Saturday evening, fog may \ndevelop Saturday night with lows mainly in the 60s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3 DESCRIPTION...\n\nAfter mainly dry days on Sunday and Monday, shower and \nthunderstorm chances will increase Monday night into Tuesday as \na wave of low pressure and associated frontal boundary crosses \nfrom the north. The timing of this disturbance as well as the \none preceding it will play a large role in determining if there\nis any potential for severe storms/flash flooding but as of \nnow, atmospheric ingredients are not appearing to be overly \nsupportive. High temperatures on Monday and Tuesday will \ngenerally be into the 70s across the north with 80s south.\n\nTemperatures will trend a little cooler through the remainder of the \nweek, although not far from seasonable averages. A few additional \nweak disturbances will bring the potential for scattered showers at \ntimes, although no day currently looks to be a washout. Given the \nongoing dry conditions in much of the region, rainfall would be \nlargely beneficial.",
